Host: RaymondZeitler

For folks in the Northern Hemisphere, September is the start of the
Academic New Year or "Back to School Month."  Families resume a busy
schedule.  We replace fun and leisure with work and study.  September,
in a nutshell, can be a hectic burden for some.  That's why it's the
perfect month for a [[Carnival]] topic on ... **Games**!

After a strenuous period of working or studying, it's important to
engage in play.  Playing games provides many benefits, two of which
are stress reduction and improvement in brain function.

Emacs comes with several diversions; they're described in the
manual at [https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Amusements.html
Amusements].  And you can find more at CategoryGames.  Do you play
any of them?  Does reading info nodes give you enjoyment?  Or do
you find that automating / configuring Emacs gives you the most
pleasure?

If you don't do games or tinker with Emacs, maybe you use Emacs for a/an

* drawing tool (with artist-mode, or other)
* e-reader
* front end to social media
* interface to a media player
* journal writing / blogging

Tell us how Emacs helps you have fun!

== Contributions ==

To join the Emacs Carnival, just write about something you do with
Emacs that's fun.  Then edit this page to add your link below, or contact
[mailto:r.zeitler@ieee.org r.zeitler@ieee.org] if you need help.
Please try to complete this by September 30, 2026 (or soon thereafter).
Thanks and have fun!

